Red Hat Advanced Cluster Security

Red Hat Advanced Cluster Security provides Kubernetes-native runtime threat detection and behavioral monitoring to protect active container workloads. The Collector component runs as a DaemonSet on every node to gather container runtime data, including process execution, network flows, and system activities.
Analyzes normal application behavior, process trees, and network connections to automatically build allow-lists.
Flags deviations such as unexpected shell spawning, unauthorized package manager execution, or container escapes in real time.
Responds to active incidents using native controls, such as alerting security teams, terminating compromised pods, or scaling suspicious deployments to zero.

The RHACS is recommended by Red hat to be installed using the operator.
Need a link between the Central and the New clusters. Init bundle is the older way replaced by the CRS as mentioned below.





Cluster Registration Secrets (CRSes) RECOMMENDED
Design: Uses a single, lightweight token dedicated only to registering a new cluster.
Security: Reduces risk because leaked tokens cannot impersonate active services; service-specific certificates are issued by Central only after registration.
Lifecycle: Fully supported in newer RHACS versions (4.10+) with configurable expiration times and cluster limits.
Usability: Easier to use across both Operator and Helm installations.


Init Bundle
Design: Contains pre-generated certificates and private keys used to initialize and authenticate secured clusters.
Security Risk: Carries a higher risk because if the bundle is compromised, the included certificates and keys can impersonate services running on the cluster.
Lifecycle: Deprecated in modern RHACS releases and scheduled for future removal in favor of CRSes.


